{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}
{\colortbl ;\red128\green128\blue128;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\f0\fs24 Chief Planning Official Endira Madraveren presented the request to establish the Mockingbird Ridge Community Development District (CDD), which would finance and maintain public infrastructure including stormwater systems, retaining walls, underground utilities, landscaping, and conservation areas. She explained that the district would be required to submit a proposed budget, a public facilities report within one year, and provide annual updates. Staff found the petition accurate, consistent with the City\rquote s comprehensive plan, and appropriate in size and function. They also determined the CDD to be a viable and compatible service provider, confirmed proper advertising, and recommended approval. \par
\par
There being no further discussion, the motion carried by roll call vote.\par
\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}
{\colortbl ;\red128\green128\blue128;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\f0\fs24 Chief Planning Official Endira Madraveren explained that the applicant is requesting amendments to the PD standards originally approved in March 2023. The changes include minor adjustments to the development layout, internal lot configurations, setbacks, roadway design, future rights\u8209?of\u8209?way, and the addition of internal setbacks applicable to both residential and commercial lots. The amendment also identifies residential signage locations and adds a definition allowing ancillary structures up to 35 feet within the 100\u8209?foot height\u8209?restricted buffer. The revisions do not alter the previously approved development maximums of 11.88 acres of commercial development and 750 residential units, and do not affect level\u8209?of\u8209?service findings or density allowances. Staff confirmed consistency with the adopted future land use and noted that sewer extension will be required, with all other utilities available. The Planning and Zoning Commission recommended approval, and staff likewise recommended approval with conditions. \par
\par
There being no further discussion the motion carried by roll call vote.\par
\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}
{\colortbl ;\red128\green128\blue128;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\f0\fs24 Deputy City Engineer Noel Cooper reported explained that the City received a FY23 Safe Streets for All Supplemental Studies grant, including $104,000 in federal funding and a $26,000 City match for a total of $130,000. City Council previously approved the agreement for these studies, which include a citywide safety plan and speed\u8209?management plan. Public engagement efforts included two community meetings, a project website, an interactive comment map, an online survey, a stakeholder advisory group that met twice, and a Council workshop held on May 12, 2026. Cooper stated that the Ocala Safety Action Plan will guide roadway\u8209?safety improvements, support development of a speed\u8209?management traffic plan, and position the City to pursue future safety\u8209?grant opportunities. Staff recommended approval.\par
\par
There being no discussion the motion carried by roll call vote.\par
\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}
{\colortbl ;\red128\green128\blue128;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\f0\fs24 Council Member Mansfield questioned why the City should be spending money on a third party rather than doing the study in-house, noting budgetary concerns. \par
\line Transit Administrator Tom Duncan explained HDR did the transportation development plan in 2023. The comprehensive operational analysis of operations will be used as a building block for the transit development plan as staff considers future long term plans.\par
\par
Growth Management Director Jeff Shrum noted the process was started earlier in the year, and there are discussions with the County as well as the Finance Department and he believes the current fiscal year will be covered in the budget. He emphasized the importance of the study to allow staff to consider the findings as they move forward.\par
\par
There being no further discussion the motion carried by role call vote.\par
\par
}

