{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}
{\colortbl ;\red128\green128\blue128;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\f0\fs24 Chief Balken reported that violent crime is slightly higher than at this time last year, with 196 incidents investigated, most of which were simple misdemeanor batteries. Property crimes have also risen modestly, with frauds making up the largest share and economic crimes detectives working to reduce those numbers. Narcotics violations and internet\u8209?related offenses represent a significant portion of reported cases. During the reporting period, the department handled over 7,800 incidents, an 11% increase, including more than 5,000 proactive calls, 227 arrests, nearly 1,000 traffic stops, and 135 crash investigations. Case highlights included an arson on Southwest Forest Street, ongoing efforts targeting street drugs and firearm violations, and progress in concluding the 2024 homicide case of Derek Brown, with one co\u8209?defendant now convicted and sentenced to life in prison.\par
\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}
{\colortbl ;\red128\green128\blue128;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\f0\fs24 Chief Wellborn reported that the fire department handled 1,110 calls during the two\u8209?week period, bringing the yearly total to 15,249. Engine and rescue units responded to high call volumes, including 162 motor vehicle accidents, two structure fires, and 97 community care calls. He noted a significant weather event on July 11-12 that left many residents without power and generated 28 emergency calls, most within the first 90 minutes of the storm. Crews responded effectively throughout the event, and no injuries were reported.\par
\par
}

