{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}
{\colortbl ;\red128\green128\blue128;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\f0\fs24 Internal Auditor Vincent Iovino reported that the city\rquote s pension processes historically lacked proper separation of duties. Although some improvements were made after the former pension manager left, significant risks remain because one person still both calculates and approves pension payments, uses a generic login for the pension bank account, and manually transfers spreadsheet\u8209?calculated payments into the system without adequate documentation or oversight. Due to the high dollar value of the pension funds, he recommended an independent forensic accounting review.\par
\par
During discussion, Council Member Hilty asked who would pay for a forensic audit; CFO Janice Mitchell said the pension boards would need to agree to it. Council Member Dreyer suggested taking the matter to the boards, and City Manager Pete Lee noted the Council can only recommend, not order, an audit. Council Pro Tem Musleh said the report didn\rquote t raise serious red flags for him, but agreed there was no harm in presenting it to the boards. Council Member Dreyer concluded by encouraging Iovino to continue pursuing audits wherever the findings lead.\par
\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}
{\colortbl ;\red128\green128\blue128;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\f0\fs24 Mr. Iovino reported that the internal audit of the recent supply\u8209?chain cybersecurity incident is complete. A draft report has been finished, legally reviewed, and shared with department heads for collaborative feedback. A final review meeting is scheduled, and he plans to present the full report at the next Council meeting. He explained that the engagement involved both advisory work and investigative work, using national cybersecurity and internal\u8209?audit standards. He noted that the breach involved a compromised trusted email source, and that within ten days of activating the incident response plan, the city identified, isolated, and recovered about nine percent of the lost funds. He praised staff for their teamwork during the response.\par
\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}
{\colortbl ;\red128\green128\blue128;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\f0\fs24 Chief Balken reported that OPD handled more than 1,700 incidents during the period, including nearly 300 reported crimes, over 200 arrests, more than 1,200 traffic stops, and investigations of 160 crashes. Since Ocala\rquote s crime rate is largely driven by retail theft, OPD has increased security efforts at major retailers and partnered with the Sheriff\rquote s Office, resulting in 48 arrests and recovery of about $7,600 in stolen merchandise. He also noted that FDOT has directed all agencies to remove license\u8209?plate reader cameras from state roadways, which OPD will complete within two weeks.\par
\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}
{\colortbl ;\red128\green128\blue128;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\f0\fs24 Chief Welborn reported that Fire Rescue handled 1,179 calls in the past two weeks, bringing the yearly total to 18,659. Rescue 4 had the highest response count, and crews managed 133 accidents, 92 fire responses, and 35 community paramedic calls. Severe weather during the period led to multiple emergency responses and significant damage to three southeast Ocala homes, though no injuries occurred. He also invited everyone to the September 11th remembrance ceremony at Veterans Park, followed by Feed the Fearless.\par
\par
\par
}

