{\colortbl ;\red128\green128\blue128;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\f0\fs24 Chief Mike Balken reported that the Ocala Police Department responded to 7,154 incidents, a 10% decrease from the previous year. The department recorded 326 reported crimes, slightly higher than the 305 reported during the same period last year. Officers made 218 arrests, conducted more than 900 traffic stops, and investigated 173 traffic crashes.\par
\par
Chief Balken advised that year-to-date crime trends show a slight increase in violent crime, while property crime continues to trend downward and societal crimes remain consistent with last year's levels. He also highlighted strong response times, noting an average Priority 1 response time of 1 minute, 18 seconds and a Priority 2 response time under 16 minutes.\par
\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}
{\colortbl ;\red128\green128\blue128;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\f0\fs24 Chief Welborn reported that during the two-week reporting period, Rescue 4 responded to 189 calls and Engine 4 responded to 145 calls. The department handled 99 motor vehicle accidents, one fire incident, and 30 Community Paramedicine/CORE calls, contributing to a year-to-date total of 22,643 calls for service.\par
\par
Chief Welborn highlighted Fire Prevention Month activities, noting that fire personnel had visited five of nine elementary schools within the city and reached more than 2,400 children through school and community education programs. He also announced the addition of 12 new members to the Special Operations Team, who will assist with specialized emergency responses including hazardous materials incidents, technical rescues, confined-space rescues, and structural collapses. Finally, he advised that Fire Rescue personnel would be participating in several upcoming community Halloween safety events.\par
\par
}

