{\colortbl ;\red128\green128\blue128;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\f0\fs24 Council Member Mansfield asked whether the project's electrical infrastructure would be entirely underground. Mr. Poole explained that a significant portion of the work would be overhead, and that the two contracts under consideration would support both overhead and underground installation activities. He added that a future contract would address additional work, including splicing and final installations.\par
\par
Mr. Mansfield also inquired whether the project included extending service into neighborhoods and ultimately to individual homes. Mr. Poole responded that contractors would complete the underground infrastructure and pull the fiber through, after which City staff could connect service from the network to individual residences.\par
\par
In response to a question about project scope, Mr. Poole stated that the current project is associated with approximately 4,500 homes, with several additional projects serving roughly 2,000 more homes. He confirmed that the work is being funded through CIP funding.\par
\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}}
{\colortbl ;\red128\green128\blue128;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\f0\fs24 Council Member Hilty questioned why a contract that was initially approved for approximately $50,000 had increased to $3 million. Mr. Poole explained that the City originally sought a contractor to address fiber splicing needs, but through discussions with Lakeland Electric learned that the selected contractor could perform a broader range of services, including splicing, aerial and underground construction, maintenance, and final buildout work. He stated that expanding the contract would help accelerate fiber network deployment and customer connections.\line\line Mr. Mansfield asked about the difference between agenda items 10D and 10E. Mr. Poole explained that the contracts cover different scopes of fiber network work, with the latter including splicing and additional construction activities. He confirmed that both contracts include underground work and that multiple contract crews would be utilized to address the significant amount of work required to advance the fiber buildout and connect customers.\line\line There being no further discussion the motion carried by roll call vote.\par
\par
}

